<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William the Silent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as William I, Prince of Orange, William of Orange · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William the Silent, Prince of Orange from 1544, was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> into the house of Nassau. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> from 1568, and the northern provinces renounced Philip in 1581. He was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in 1584 and is honoured as the father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> nation and founder of the house of Orange-Nassau.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-silent.jpg" alt="William the Silent" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adriaen Thomasz. Key · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_I,_Prince_of_Orange_by_Adriaen_Thomasz._Key_Rijksmuseum_Amsterdam_SK-A-3148.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 24 April 1533 at Dillenburg Castle, the eldest of twelve children of Count William I of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> and Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-stolberg">Juliana of Stolberg</a>. The family was devout and he was raised a Lutheran. A ward of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, educated under the Emperor's sister Mary, he became a favourite of the imperial family, was made a cavalry captain in 1551 and in 1555 commanded one of the Emperor's armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>. In November 1555 Charles leaned on his shoulder at the ceremony in which he gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. William carried the imperial insignia to Ferdinand in 1556 and was a Spanish signatory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> in 1559.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1559 Philip appointed him stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland and Utrecht, and in 1561 of Franche-Comté. He soon became a leading figure of the opposition in the Council of State, with Philip de Montmorency, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-de-montmorency-count-of-horn">Count of Hoorn</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</a>, who sought more power for themselves and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> nobility against the government of Granvelle and others, and complained of the number of Spaniards in government. He also objected to the persecution of Protestants. Brought up a Lutheran and later a Catholic, he was very religious but favoured freedom of religion for all.</p><p>In October 1568 he led a large army into Brabant, but Alba avoided a decisive battle, disorder broke out in the army, money ran short, and William turned back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Later invasion plans came to little for lack of money and support, but he remained popular, helped by a pamphlet campaign claiming that he fought not the king but foreign governors and troops. In March 1580 Philip declared him an outlaw and promised 25,000 crowns to anyone who killed him; William answered with his Apology, written by Villiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Balthasar Gérard, a Burgundian Catholic who regarded William as a traitor, learned of the reward in 1581. In May 1584 he presented himself to William as a French nobleman and gained his trust, and William was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in July. He was buried in the New Church at Delft, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, where the Nassau family was traditionally buried, was under royal control. Hendrik de Keyser and his son Pieter made a new monument in 1623.</p><p>William married four times. His eldest son Philip William succeeded him as Prince of Orange, though held hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> became stadtholder. Frederick Henry, his youngest son, inherited the title in 1625. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> became formally independent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Peace of Münster</a> in 1648. In the Netherlands William is widely regarded as the principal founding father and is called Father of the Fatherland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William the Silent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William the Silent</b>, Prince of Orange and stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland and Utrecht, led armed resistance from 1568, and in 1572 the capture of Brielle by the Sea Beggars allowed the rebels to take most towns of Holland and Zeeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The States appointed him, and nearly always chose a prince of the House of Orange-Nassau, beginning with <b>William the Silent</b>, so that the office became effectively hereditary.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>At Jodoigne, the royal Habsburg army of the Duke of Alba, governor of the Netherlands, defeated a Protestant rebel army led by <b>William the Silent</b>, Prince of Orange, who had to abandon his plans to invade the Habsburg Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-20">20 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Walram's line produced King Adolf of Nassau, elected in 1292, and later the grand dukes of Luxembourg; Otto's line produced <b>William the Silent</b> and the house of Orange-Nassau, today's Dutch royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau">County of Nassau</a></li><li><blockquote>William the Rich introduced the Reformation; his son <b>William the Silent</b>, born at Dillenburg in 1533, inherited Orange.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Botho III's daughter Juliana of Stolberg was mother of <b>William the Silent</b>, and his sons introduced the Reformation after his death in 1538.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-stolberg">County of Stolberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1566" aria-label="Dillenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William the Silent"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5447 5761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5447 5761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1566">What was here in 1566? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
